1
00:00:07,780 --> 00:00:14,820
So he spoke; they were all hushed in silence,
held fast under the spell, through the shadowy halls.

2
00:00:14,820 --> 00:00:18,060
Then Alcinous answered him in turn, and said:

3
00:00:18,060 --> 00:00:21,800
"Odysseus — now that you have come
to my house with its floor of bronze,

4
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:24,720
its high roof, I do not think you will be driven wandering

5
00:00:24,720 --> 00:00:28,600
back again as you return, however much you have suffered.

6
00:00:28,600 --> 00:00:32,120
And to each man of you I say this, laying it on you —

7
00:00:32,120 --> 00:00:37,420
you who in my halls forever drink the elders'
fire-bright wine, and listen to the singer:

8
00:00:37,420 --> 00:00:40,420
clothing for the guest lies packed
already in the polished chest,

9
00:00:40,420 --> 00:00:45,820
with gold of intricate work and all the other gifts
the counselors of the Phaeacians brought him here.

10
00:00:45,820 --> 00:00:49,840
Come now, let us give him besides
a great tripod and a cauldron,

11
00:00:49,840 --> 00:00:53,320
each man of us; and we in turn will
gather it back from the people:

12
00:00:53,320 --> 00:00:57,540
it is hard on one man to give
so freely without repayment."

13
00:00:57,540 --> 00:01:04,060
So spoke Alcinous, and his word pleased them well,
and they went home to sleep, each man to his own house.

14
00:01:04,060 --> 00:01:07,480
When Dawn appeared, the early-born, with fingers of rose,

15
00:01:07,480 --> 00:01:11,320
they hurried to the ship, carrying
the bronze that does honor to a man,

16
00:01:11,320 --> 00:01:14,060
and the sacred strength of Alcinous stowed it well,

17
00:01:14,060 --> 00:01:17,400
going himself through the ship,
under the benches, so that it might not

18
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:20,820
hamper any of the crew at the rowing,
when they leaned to the oars.

19
00:01:20,820 --> 00:01:24,880
Then they went to the house of
Alcinous and saw to the feast.

20
00:01:24,880 --> 00:01:27,300
For them the sacred strength of Alcinous slaughtered an ox

21
00:01:27,300 --> 00:01:31,460
to Zeus of the black cloud,
the son of Cronos, lord of all.

22
00:01:31,460 --> 00:01:37,400
They burned the thigh-cuts and shared the glorious feast,
rejoicing; and among them sang the godlike singer,

23
00:01:37,400 --> 00:01:43,820
Demodocus, honored among the people. But Odysseus
kept turning his head toward the blazing sun,

24
00:01:43,820 --> 00:01:47,720
willing it to set: for now his
whole heart strained toward home.

25
00:01:47,720 --> 00:01:50,500
As when a man aches for his supper — all day long

26
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:54,400
his two wine-dark oxen have dragged the
joint of plow through fallow ground,

27
00:01:54,400 --> 00:01:56,920
and welcome to him is the setting of the sun's light,

28
00:01:56,920 --> 00:02:00,440
the going home to supper,
and his knees buckle as he walks:

29
00:02:00,440 --> 00:02:03,800
so welcome to Odysseus was the setting of the sun's light.

30
00:02:03,800 --> 00:02:09,180
At once he spoke among the oar-loving Phaeacians,
and to Alcinous above all he declared his word:

31
00:02:09,180 --> 00:02:12,300
"Lord Alcinous, most renowned among all peoples:

32
00:02:12,300 --> 00:02:16,660
pour the offering, and send me on my
way unharmed. And yourselves — farewell.

33
00:02:16,660 --> 00:02:19,560
For now all that my own heart wished is brought to pass:

34
00:02:19,560 --> 00:02:22,020
a sending, and gifts of friendship. May the gods of heaven

35
00:02:22,020 --> 00:02:27,860
make them a blessing! And may I come home and find
my blameless wife there, with those I love unharmed.

36
00:02:27,860 --> 00:02:30,540
And you who stay: may you be the joy of your wedded wives

37
00:02:30,540 --> 00:02:33,300
and of your children; and may the gods grant prosperity

38
00:02:33,300 --> 00:02:37,300
of every kind, and no evil come
to dwell among your people."

39
00:02:37,300 --> 00:02:43,200
So he spoke, and they all approved it and urged that
the guest be sent home, since he had spoken rightly.

40
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:45,760
And then the strength of Alcinous said to the herald:

41
00:02:45,760 --> 00:02:51,860
"Pontonous, mix the bowl, and serve out the wine to
all in the hall, so that with a prayer to father Zeus

42
00:02:51,860 --> 00:02:54,880
we may send the guest to his own fatherland."

43
00:02:54,880 --> 00:02:58,320
So he spoke, and Pontonous mixed the honey-hearted wine

44
00:02:58,320 --> 00:03:04,480
and served it to all in turn; and they poured to the
blessed gods who hold wide heaven, there where each man

45
00:03:04,480 --> 00:03:10,520
sat. But noble Odysseus rose to his feet
and set the two-handled cup in Arete's hand,

46
00:03:10,520 --> 00:03:13,540
and lifting his voice he spoke winged words to her:

47
00:03:13,540 --> 00:03:16,560
"Fare well, my queen, through all the years, until old age

48
00:03:16,560 --> 00:03:20,380
comes, and death, which stand over every one of us.

49
00:03:20,380 --> 00:03:27,080
I go now. And you — have joy of this house,
of your children and your people and Alcinous the king."

50
00:03:27,080 --> 00:03:30,620
So speaking, noble Odysseus stepped across the threshold,

51
00:03:30,620 --> 00:03:37,040
and the strength of Alcinous sent the herald with him
to lead him to the fast ship and the shore of the sea;

52
00:03:37,040 --> 00:03:42,720
and Arete sent slave-women along with him,
one carrying a fresh-washed mantle and a tunic,

53
00:03:42,720 --> 00:03:49,140
another she sent to bring the close-packed chest,
and a third carried bread and red wine.

54
00:03:49,140 --> 00:03:54,960
And when they had come down to the ship and the sea,
the lordly escorts took it all at once and stowed it

55
00:03:54,960 --> 00:04:01,060
in the hollow ship, all the food and drink;
and for Odysseus they spread a blanket and a linen sheet

56
00:04:01,060 --> 00:04:04,140
on the half-deck of the hollow ship,
so that he might sleep

57
00:04:04,140 --> 00:04:08,800
unwaking, at the stern.
And he came aboard himself and lay down

58
00:04:08,800 --> 00:04:12,440
in silence. And they took their
places at the oarlocks, each in

59
00:04:12,440 --> 00:04:15,680
order, and loosed the cable from the pierced stone.

60
00:04:15,680 --> 00:04:19,080
And the moment they leaned back and
tossed the sea with the oar-blade,

61
00:04:19,080 --> 00:04:25,220
that moment sweet sleep fell upon his eyelids —
unwaking, sweetest sleep, and most like death.

62
00:04:25,220 --> 00:04:28,280
And the ship — as on the plain a team of four stallions

63
00:04:28,280 --> 00:04:34,260
springs forward all together under the lash's strokes,
and rearing high they swiftly accomplish their road:

64
00:04:34,260 --> 00:04:37,500
so her stern reared and plunged, and behind her the great

65
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:40,800
dark-gleaming wave of the loud-roaring sea rushed on.

66
00:04:40,800 --> 00:04:44,780
And she ran sure and steady,
never yielding: not even the hawk

67
00:04:44,780 --> 00:04:48,820
could have kept beside her,
the falcon, nimblest of winged things.

68
00:04:48,820 --> 00:04:54,320
So running lightly she cut the waves of the sea,
carrying a man with counsels like the gods' —

69
00:04:54,320 --> 00:04:57,960
a man who before had suffered pains in plenty, heart-deep,

70
00:04:57,960 --> 00:05:00,580
cutting through wars of men and the grinding waves,

71
00:05:00,580 --> 00:05:04,460
and who now slept without a tremor,
all he had suffered forgotten.

72
00:05:04,460 --> 00:05:07,420
When the brightest of stars was rising, the one that comes

73
00:05:07,420 --> 00:05:10,800
above all others announcing the light of early-born Dawn,

74
00:05:10,800 --> 00:05:13,920
then the seagoing ship was drawing near the island.

75
00:05:13,920 --> 00:05:21,280
There is a harbor of Phorcys, the old man of the sea,
in the land of Ithaca: two jutting headlands in it,

76
00:05:21,280 --> 00:05:24,500
sheer on the seaward side but sloping down toward harbor,

77
00:05:24,500 --> 00:05:27,460
which shelter it from the great
waves the hard-blowing winds raise

78
00:05:27,460 --> 00:05:30,980
outside; and within, the well-benched ships ride

79
00:05:30,980 --> 00:05:34,800
without a mooring-line,
once they have reached anchoring-ground.

80
00:05:34,800 --> 00:05:40,700
At the head of the harbor is a long-leafed olive,
and near it a lovely cave, full of sea-mist,

81
00:05:40,700 --> 00:05:43,680
sacred to the nymphs that men call Naiads.

82
00:05:43,680 --> 00:05:49,040
In it are mixing-bowls and two-handled jars of
stone; and there the bees lay up their honey.

83
00:05:49,040 --> 00:05:55,040
And in it are long looms of stone, where the
nymphs weave cloth of sea-purple, a wonder to see;

84
00:05:55,040 --> 00:05:58,520
and ever-flowing waters. It has two doors:

85
00:05:58,520 --> 00:06:04,740
one toward the North Wind, by which men come down;
the other, toward the South, is for the gods: by that way

86
00:06:04,740 --> 00:06:07,720
men do not enter — it is the immortals' road.

87
00:06:07,720 --> 00:06:13,980
In there they rowed, knowing the place of old.
And the ship ran up on the shore a full half of her length,

88
00:06:13,980 --> 00:06:17,820
at speed: such was the drive of the rowers' arms.

89
00:06:17,820 --> 00:06:20,880
And they stepped from the well-benched ship onto dry land,

90
00:06:20,880 --> 00:06:26,200
and first they lifted Odysseus out of the hollow ship,
linen sheet and shining blanket and all,

91
00:06:26,200 --> 00:06:29,580
and laid him down on the sand, still mastered by sleep;

92
00:06:29,580 --> 00:06:32,100
and they lifted out the goods that the lordly Phaeacians

93
00:06:32,100 --> 00:06:36,620
had given him for his journey home,
through the grace of great-hearted Athena.

94
00:06:36,620 --> 00:06:41,340
These they set in a heap by the trunk of the olive,
out of the path, for fear some passer-by

95
00:06:41,340 --> 00:06:44,080
might come and pillage them before Odysseus woke;

96
00:06:44,080 --> 00:06:47,820
and they themselves set off for
home again. But the shaker of earth

97
00:06:47,820 --> 00:06:50,580
had not forgotten the threats he had made at the first

98
00:06:50,580 --> 00:06:54,800
against godlike Odysseus;
and he asked out the design of Zeus:

99
00:06:54,800 --> 00:07:00,020
"Father Zeus, I shall have honor no longer among the
deathless gods, when mortals honor me not at all —

100
00:07:00,020 --> 00:07:03,500
the Phaeacians, who are of my very own line.

101
00:07:03,500 --> 00:07:06,540
I said just now that Odysseus
should suffer evils in plenty

102
00:07:06,540 --> 00:07:10,160
before he came home — his homecoming itself I never

103
00:07:10,160 --> 00:07:13,480
wholly denied him, once you had
promised it and bowed your head —

104
00:07:13,480 --> 00:07:16,880
and look: they have carried him
sleeping over the sea in their fast ship

105
00:07:16,880 --> 00:07:23,700
and set him down in Ithaca, and given him gifts past
speaking, bronze and gold in heaps and woven clothing,

106
00:07:23,700 --> 00:07:25,880
more than Odysseus would ever have carried out of Troy

107
00:07:25,880 --> 00:07:30,000
had he come home untouched with
his fair share of the spoils."

108
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:32,620
Then Zeus who gathers the clouds answered him:

109
00:07:32,620 --> 00:07:36,500
"Shame on you, wide-ruling shaker
of earth — what a thing to say.

110
00:07:36,500 --> 00:07:41,820
The gods do not dishonor you: it would be a hard
thing to pelt our eldest and best with dishonor.

111
00:07:41,820 --> 00:07:45,280
As for men — if any man,
yielding to his own force and power,

112
00:07:45,280 --> 00:07:49,720
fails to honor you, vengeance
is yours, now and always after.

113
00:07:49,720 --> 00:07:52,740
Do as you will: whatever is dear to your heart."

114
00:07:52,740 --> 00:07:54,980
Then Poseidon the earth-shaker answered him:

115
00:07:54,980 --> 00:07:58,380
"I would have done it at once,
lord of the black cloud, as you say;

116
00:07:58,380 --> 00:08:01,760
but I always shrink before your anger, and I avoid it.

117
00:08:01,760 --> 00:08:07,160
That beautiful ship of the Phaeacians,
coming home over the misted sea from her sending —

118
00:08:07,160 --> 00:08:10,500
I will smash her, so that they hold off at last and cease

119
00:08:10,500 --> 00:08:14,940
their escorting of men; and I will pile
a great mountain round their city."

120
00:08:14,940 --> 00:08:18,020
Then Zeus who gathers the clouds answered him:

121
00:08:18,020 --> 00:08:20,700
"Friend — hear how it seems best to my own mind.

122
00:08:20,700 --> 00:08:26,220
When all the people are watching her from the city
as she drives in, turn her to stone close offshore —

123
00:08:26,220 --> 00:08:29,140
a stone in the likeness of a
fast ship, so that all mankind

124
00:08:29,140 --> 00:08:32,860
may marvel; and pile the great mountain round their city."

125
00:08:32,860 --> 00:08:35,040
And when Poseidon the earth-shaker heard it,

126
00:08:35,040 --> 00:08:38,620
he went his way to Scheria,
where the Phaeacians are born and bred,

127
00:08:38,620 --> 00:08:42,720
and there he waited. And she
came close in, the seagoing ship,

128
00:08:42,720 --> 00:08:46,400
running lightly on. And the shaker of earth came near her,

129
00:08:46,400 --> 00:08:49,240
and turned her to stone, and rooted her to the sea-floor

130
00:08:49,240 --> 00:08:53,840
with one downward stroke of the
flat of his hand. And he was gone.

131
00:08:53,840 --> 00:08:59,960
And they spoke to one another with winged words,
the Phaeacians of the long oars, men famed for ships;

132
00:08:59,960 --> 00:09:02,940
and thus one would say, with a glance at his neighbor:

133
00:09:02,940 --> 00:09:06,100
"Ah me — who has shackled our fast ship on the open sea

134
00:09:06,100 --> 00:09:09,720
as she drove for home —
in full view, every timber of her?"

135
00:09:09,720 --> 00:09:13,120
So they would say — knowing
nothing of how it had been done.

136
00:09:13,120 --> 00:09:15,400
And Alcinous addressed them all, and said:

137
00:09:15,400 --> 00:09:17,760
"Ah, then it comes home to me — the old prophecy

138
00:09:17,760 --> 00:09:21,200
of my father, who would tell us that
Poseidon looked on us with anger

139
00:09:21,200 --> 00:09:24,700
because we are safe escorts of
all men without distinction.

140
00:09:24,700 --> 00:09:27,880
He said that one day,
as a beautiful ship of the Phaeacian men

141
00:09:27,880 --> 00:09:30,800
came home over the misted sea from such a sending,

142
00:09:30,800 --> 00:09:34,560
the god would smash her, and pile
a great mountain round our city.

143
00:09:34,560 --> 00:09:38,240
So the old man said. And now it is all being accomplished.

144
00:09:38,240 --> 00:09:41,580
Come then — all of us — do exactly as I say:

145
00:09:41,580 --> 00:09:46,880
cease your escorting of mortals, whoever comes to
our city hereafter; and to Poseidon let us sacrifice

146
00:09:46,880 --> 00:09:52,980
twelve chosen bulls, in hope he may take pity
and not pile the long mountain round our city."

147
00:09:52,980 --> 00:09:56,100
So he spoke; and they were afraid,
and made the bulls ready.

148
00:09:56,100 --> 00:10:01,420
So they were praying to the lord Poseidon,
the leaders and counselors of the land of the Phaeacians,

149
00:10:01,420 --> 00:10:04,360
standing around the altar. — And noble Odysseus woke

150
00:10:04,360 --> 00:10:07,840
out of sleep in his own fathers'
land — and did not know it,

151
00:10:07,840 --> 00:10:12,160
so long had he been gone. For the
goddess had poured a mist around him,

152
00:10:12,160 --> 00:10:15,420
Pallas Athena, daughter of Zeus,
so that she might make him

153
00:10:15,420 --> 00:10:18,520
unknowable to all, and tell him everything through,

154
00:10:18,520 --> 00:10:21,980
lest his wife should know him,
and his townsmen, and his friends,

155
00:10:21,980 --> 00:10:24,620
before the suitors paid for all their trespass.

156
00:10:24,620 --> 00:10:27,400
And so to their lord all things showed strange —

157
00:10:27,400 --> 00:10:31,580
the long straight paths, the anchorages
that take ships from every wind,

158
00:10:31,580 --> 00:10:34,820
the sheer-climbing rocks and the trees in full leaf.

159
00:10:34,820 --> 00:10:40,540
He sprang up and stood, and stared at his own fatherland,
and then he groaned aloud, and struck both thighs

160
00:10:40,540 --> 00:10:44,240
with the flats of his hands, and spoke in raw lament:

161
00:10:44,240 --> 00:10:47,800
"Ah me — whose country have I come to now? What men's?

162
00:10:47,800 --> 00:10:53,600
Are they violent, savage, without justice,
or kind to strangers, with hearts that fear the gods?

163
00:10:53,600 --> 00:10:56,420
Where am I to carry all this treasure? Where am I myself

164
00:10:56,420 --> 00:11:00,060
to wander? Would it had stayed
with the Phaeacians, all of it,

165
00:11:00,060 --> 00:11:02,660
there where it was — and I had
come to some other mighty king,

166
00:11:02,660 --> 00:11:06,300
who would have hosted me kindly
and sent me on my way home.

167
00:11:06,300 --> 00:11:12,020
As it is, I have nowhere to put it — and I will
not leave it here, to fall as plunder to other men.

168
00:11:12,020 --> 00:11:15,540
Shame on them — not righteous through
and through, then, and not just,

169
00:11:15,540 --> 00:11:21,460
the leaders and counselors of the Phaeacians,
who carried me off to a strange country! They said, truly,

170
00:11:21,460 --> 00:11:25,280
they would bring me to clear-skied
Ithaca — and did not do it.

171
00:11:25,280 --> 00:11:31,840
May Zeus of Suppliants pay them for it, who watches
over all mankind, and punishes whoever goes astray.

172
00:11:31,840 --> 00:11:34,980
But come — let me count my goods and look them over,

173
00:11:34,980 --> 00:11:37,460
in case they carried something
off with them in the hollow ship."

174
00:11:37,460 --> 00:11:41,660
So speaking he counted up the
surpassingly beautiful tripods

175
00:11:41,660 --> 00:11:45,580
and the cauldrons, and the gold,
and the fine woven clothing —

176
00:11:45,580 --> 00:11:49,880
and missed not one thing. Then,
mourning for his fatherland,

177
00:11:49,880 --> 00:11:55,540
he crept along the shore of the loud-roaring sea,
grieving without pause. And Athena came near him

178
00:11:55,540 --> 00:12:01,740
in the likeness of a young man, a herder of sheep,
delicate-skinned, such as the sons of kings are,

179
00:12:01,740 --> 00:12:08,940
a fine-made double cloak hanging from her shoulders,
sandals under her shining feet, a javelin in her hand.

180
00:12:08,940 --> 00:12:12,240
Odysseus rejoiced at the sight
of her, and came to meet her,

181
00:12:12,240 --> 00:12:15,500
and lifting his voice he spoke winged words to her:

182
00:12:15,500 --> 00:12:18,480
"Friend — since you are the first I meet in this country,

183
00:12:18,480 --> 00:12:22,920
hail to you! And may you come at me with no evil purpose:

184
00:12:22,920 --> 00:12:29,260
save these things, and save me. To you as to a god
I pray, and I come to your knees in supplication.

185
00:12:29,260 --> 00:12:33,460
And tell me this too, honestly, so that I may know it:

186
00:12:33,460 --> 00:12:37,220
what land is this? what people? what men are born here?

187
00:12:37,220 --> 00:12:40,080
Is it some clear-skied island — or is it a headland

188
00:12:40,080 --> 00:12:43,720
of the rich-soiled mainland, shelving down into the sea?"

189
00:12:43,720 --> 00:12:47,260
Then the goddess spoke to him again, gray-eyed Athena:

190
00:12:47,260 --> 00:12:53,400
"You are a fool, stranger — or you have come from far
away — if you ask about this land. It is not so very

191
00:12:53,400 --> 00:12:58,900
nameless as all that: very many men know her,
both all who live toward the dawn and the sun

192
00:12:58,900 --> 00:13:02,520
and all who live back westward, toward the misty dark.

193
00:13:02,520 --> 00:13:09,120
Rough she is, truly — no ground for driving horses —
yet not too poor a land, though no wide one either:

194
00:13:09,120 --> 00:13:14,860
grain grows in her past speaking, and wine as well,
and the rain never fails her, or the fresh dew;

195
00:13:14,860 --> 00:13:17,820
good pasture she is for goats and cattle; there is timber

196
00:13:17,820 --> 00:13:21,460
of every kind, and watering-places
that flow the year round.

197
00:13:21,460 --> 00:13:25,220
And so, stranger, the name of
Ithaca has reached even to Troy —

198
00:13:25,220 --> 00:13:28,660
and that, they say, lies far from this Achaean land."

199
00:13:28,660 --> 00:13:34,760
So she spoke; and much-enduring noble Odysseus was glad,
rejoicing in the land of his fathers, at the word

200
00:13:34,760 --> 00:13:38,120
of Pallas Athena, daughter of aegis-bearing Zeus.

201
00:13:38,120 --> 00:13:41,220
And lifting his voice he spoke winged words to her —

202
00:13:41,220 --> 00:13:44,860
yet he did not speak the truth:
he swallowed the word back down,

203
00:13:44,860 --> 00:13:48,000
forever steering, in his breast, the mind of many profits:

204
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:53,060
"I heard tell of Ithaca even in wide Crete,
far over the sea. And now I have come here myself

205
00:13:53,060 --> 00:13:56,560
with all this treasure —
and left as much again for my children —

206
00:13:56,560 --> 00:14:02,520
a fugitive: for I killed the dear son of Idomeneus,
Orsilochus the swift-footed, who in wide Crete

207
00:14:02,520 --> 00:14:05,260
outran all bread-eating men with his quick feet.

208
00:14:05,260 --> 00:14:10,540
He meant to strip me of all my Trojan plunder,
the winnings I had suffered pains for, heart-deep,

209
00:14:10,540 --> 00:14:15,420
cutting through wars of men and the grinding waves —
all because I would not oblige his father and serve

210
00:14:15,420 --> 00:14:17,680
under him at Troy, but led my own command.

211
00:14:17,680 --> 00:14:21,300
So I struck him with my bronze-tipped spear as he came in

212
00:14:21,300 --> 00:14:25,740
from the fields — lay in ambush
by the road with one companion.

213
00:14:25,740 --> 00:14:30,920
Black night held the sky that night:
no living soul saw us; I took his life unseen.

214
00:14:30,920 --> 00:14:35,560
And when I had cut him down with the sharp bronze,
I went straight to a ship of the lordly Phoenicians

215
00:14:35,560 --> 00:14:39,280
and begged their grace, and gave
them plunder to content their hearts:

216
00:14:39,280 --> 00:14:45,260
I asked them to take me aboard and set me down at Pylos,
or at shining Elis, where the Epeians hold power.

217
00:14:45,260 --> 00:14:50,520
But the wind's force beat them off that course —
much against their will: they had no wish to cheat me —

218
00:14:50,520 --> 00:14:53,200
and driven wide, we came here in the night.

219
00:14:53,200 --> 00:14:58,660
We rowed hard into the harbor, and not one of us
had a thought of supper, hungry for it as we were:

220
00:14:58,660 --> 00:15:02,400
we simply stepped ashore, all of us,
and lay down as we landed.

221
00:15:02,400 --> 00:15:08,020
Then sweet sleep came over me, worn out as I was,
and they lifted my goods out of the hollow ship

222
00:15:08,020 --> 00:15:14,840
and set them down where I myself lay on the sands,
and boarded, and went off toward well-peopled Sidon —

223
00:15:14,840 --> 00:15:17,700
and I was left behind, my heart aching."

224
00:15:17,700 --> 00:15:21,300
So he spoke; and the goddess smiled, gray-eyed Athena,

225
00:15:21,300 --> 00:15:24,620
and stroked him with her hand —
and now her body was a woman's,

226
00:15:24,620 --> 00:15:31,340
beautiful and tall, and skilled in glorious handwork —
and lifting her voice she spoke winged words to him:

227
00:15:31,340 --> 00:15:35,200
"A sharp one he would have to be,
a master-thief, to slip past you

228
00:15:35,200 --> 00:15:38,920
in all your manifold tricks —
even a god, if one should meet you.

229
00:15:38,920 --> 00:15:42,900
You hard man — shifting-crafted,
glutton for guile — so not even

230
00:15:42,900 --> 00:15:45,460
here, in your own country,
would you leave off the cheating

231
00:15:45,460 --> 00:15:49,320
and the thieving tales that are dear
to you from the ground of your heart!

232
00:15:49,320 --> 00:15:55,700
But come, no more of this now. We are both old hands
at profit: you are far the best of all mortal men

233
00:15:55,700 --> 00:15:58,760
in counsel and in words, and I am among all the gods

234
00:15:58,760 --> 00:16:06,440
and famed for cunning and for profit. And yet you did
not know Pallas Athena, daughter of Zeus — I who stand

235
00:16:06,440 --> 00:16:13,740
beside you in every labor, always, and keep watch over you,
and who made you dear to all the Phaeacians.

236
00:16:13,740 --> 00:16:18,460
Now I have come here to weave a design with you,
and to hide the goods the lordly Phaeacians

237
00:16:18,460 --> 00:16:22,540
gave you for your journey home —
by my own thought and counsel —

238
00:16:22,540 --> 00:16:24,840
and to tell you all the cares that fate must fill

239
00:16:24,840 --> 00:16:29,700
your well-built house with.
You must endure them — there is no choice —

240
00:16:29,700 --> 00:16:37,100
and tell no one, no man and no woman, none of them all,
that it is you, come home from your wandering. No:

241
00:16:37,100 --> 00:16:41,300
in silence suffer your many pains,
and submit to men's violence."

242
00:16:41,300 --> 00:16:44,040
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered her:

243
00:16:44,040 --> 00:16:48,000
"Hard it is, goddess, for a mortal
man who meets you to know you,

244
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:51,880
however knowing he be:
for you take what likeness you will.

245
00:16:51,880 --> 00:16:57,080
This I know well — that you were kind to me once,
as long as we sons of the Achaeans made war at Troy;

246
00:16:57,080 --> 00:17:04,100
but after we had sacked Priam's steep city,
and gone to our ships, and a god scattered the Achaeans,

247
00:17:04,100 --> 00:17:07,780
from then on I never saw you,
daughter of Zeus — never marked you

248
00:17:07,780 --> 00:17:10,800
setting foot on my ship to beat some grief away from me.

249
00:17:10,800 --> 00:17:17,540
No: I wandered on, always carrying a heart torn open
in my breast, until the gods released me from evil —

250
00:17:17,540 --> 00:17:20,780
until that day, in the rich land of the Phaeacian men,

251
00:17:20,780 --> 00:17:24,600
when you heartened me with your words,
and led me to the city yourself.

252
00:17:24,600 --> 00:17:30,480
Now I beg you by your father — for I cannot think I
have truly come to clear-skied Ithaca: it is some other

253
00:17:30,480 --> 00:17:35,920
country I am turned loose in, and you are mocking
me when you tell me this, to lead my wits astray —

254
00:17:35,920 --> 00:17:39,520
tell me: am I really come to my own dear fatherland?"

255
00:17:39,520 --> 00:17:42,540
Then the goddess answered him, gray-eyed Athena:

256
00:17:42,540 --> 00:17:45,200
"Always the same — that mind in your breast!

257
00:17:45,200 --> 00:17:47,920
And that is why I cannot forsake you in your misery:

258
00:17:47,920 --> 00:17:51,400
because you are civil,
and quick-witted, and steady-souled.

259
00:17:51,400 --> 00:17:53,400
Gladly would any other man, come home from wandering,

260
00:17:53,400 --> 00:18:00,420
have rushed to his hall to see his children and his wife;
but you — you have no wish yet to learn or to ask

261
00:18:00,420 --> 00:18:05,560
until you have made trial of your wife besides —
who sits as ever in your halls, and always in misery

262
00:18:05,560 --> 00:18:08,860
her nights waste away, and her days, in tears.

263
00:18:08,860 --> 00:18:14,360
I, though — I never doubted it: I knew in my heart
you would come home with all your companions lost.

264
00:18:14,360 --> 00:18:20,360
But I had no mind, you see, to fight Poseidon,
my father's brother, who set the grudge in his heart,

265
00:18:20,360 --> 00:18:22,560
raging because you blinded his dear son.

266
00:18:22,560 --> 00:18:26,800
Come — I will show you the ground-plan
of Ithaca, so you will believe.

267
00:18:26,800 --> 00:18:29,760
This is the harbor of Phorcys, the old man of the sea;

268
00:18:29,760 --> 00:18:36,140
and this, at the head of the harbor, the long-leafed olive;
and near it the lovely cave, full of sea-mist,

269
00:18:36,140 --> 00:18:41,460
sacred to the nymphs that men call Naiads —
there is the wide vaulted cavern where you used to offer

270
00:18:41,460 --> 00:18:47,800
the nymphs so many perfect hecatombs; and this
is the mountain Neriton, clothed in its forest."

271
00:18:47,800 --> 00:18:52,080
So speaking, the goddess scattered
the mist — and the land showed plain.

272
00:18:52,080 --> 00:18:58,380
Then much-enduring noble Odysseus rejoiced —
he exulted in his own land, and kissed the grain-giving earth,

273
00:18:58,380 --> 00:19:01,700
and at once he prayed to the nymphs, lifting up his hands:

274
00:19:01,700 --> 00:19:07,340
"Nymphs of the springs, daughters of Zeus —
I told myself I would never see you again. Now take my greeting

275
00:19:07,340 --> 00:19:11,800
and my gentle prayers. And we will
give you gifts, as in the old days,

276
00:19:11,800 --> 00:19:15,200
if the daughter of Zeus,
the driver of spoils, in her grace

277
00:19:15,200 --> 00:19:19,540
lets me live, and brings my dear son to his manhood."

278
00:19:19,540 --> 00:19:24,180
Then the goddess spoke to him again, gray-eyed Athena:

279
00:19:24,180 --> 00:19:27,620
"Take heart: let none of that weigh on your mind.

280
00:19:27,620 --> 00:19:30,700
Rather let us stow the goods in the
innermost hollow of the wondrous

281
00:19:30,700 --> 00:19:35,220
cave, this instant, so that they stay there safe for you;

282
00:19:35,220 --> 00:19:38,860
and let us two take counsel how the best may come about."

283
00:19:38,860 --> 00:19:44,960
So speaking, the goddess plunged into the misty cave,
searching along its hiding-holes; and Odysseus

284
00:19:44,960 --> 00:19:49,140
carried everything close in —
the gold, the wear-proof bronze,

285
00:19:49,140 --> 00:19:52,060
and the well-made clothing the Phaeacians gave him.

286
00:19:52,060 --> 00:20:00,080
He laid them carefully by; and a stone was set at the
door by Pallas Athena, daughter of aegis-bearing Zeus.

287
00:20:00,080 --> 00:20:03,840
Then the two of them sat down
at the trunk of the sacred olive

288
00:20:03,840 --> 00:20:06,680
and plotted death for the overweening suitors.

289
00:20:06,680 --> 00:20:09,960
And the goddess began to speak, gray-eyed Athena:

290
00:20:09,960 --> 00:20:14,420
"Zeus-born son of Laertes, Odysseus of the many devices —

291
00:20:14,420 --> 00:20:17,340
consider how you will lay your
hands on the shameless suitors,

292
00:20:17,340 --> 00:20:20,180
who for three years now have lorded it through your hall,

293
00:20:20,180 --> 00:20:23,760
courting your godlike wife and offering bride-gifts;

294
00:20:23,760 --> 00:20:27,680
and she — grieving without pause
for your homecoming, heart-deep,

295
00:20:27,680 --> 00:20:34,480
gives hope to all, and makes promises to every man,
sending out messages — while her mind wills otherwise."

296
00:20:34,480 --> 00:20:37,210
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered her:

297
00:20:37,210 --> 00:20:44,370
"Ah, gods — so I was to have perished by the same
evil doom as Agamemnon, Atreus' son, in my own halls,

298
00:20:44,370 --> 00:20:48,210
had you not told me each thing rightly,
goddess, in its measure.

299
00:20:48,210 --> 00:20:51,430
Come then — weave a design, how I shall pay them back;

300
00:20:51,430 --> 00:20:55,070
and stand beside me yourself, breathing bold fury into me,

301
00:20:55,070 --> 00:20:58,230
as on the day we loosed the shining head-veil of Troy.

302
00:20:58,230 --> 00:21:03,810
Were you to stand by me with that same fire,
gray-eyes, I would fight three hundred men together —

303
00:21:03,810 --> 00:21:07,730
with you, sovereign goddess,
when you choose in your heart to help me."

304
00:21:07,730 --> 00:21:10,410
Then the goddess answered him, gray-eyed Athena:

305
00:21:10,410 --> 00:21:13,790
"Oh, I will be with you — be sure
of it — you will not slip my sight

306
00:21:13,790 --> 00:21:17,550
when the hour comes and we are at this work.
And more than one, I think,

307
00:21:17,550 --> 00:21:22,630
of the suitor-men who eat away your living will
spatter the vast floor with his blood and brains.

308
00:21:22,630 --> 00:21:25,410
But come: I will make you unknowable to every mortal.

309
00:21:25,410 --> 00:21:28,130
I will wither the fine skin on your supple limbs;

310
00:21:28,130 --> 00:21:31,350
I will strip the fair hair from
your head; I will wrap you in rags

311
00:21:31,350 --> 00:21:37,650
such that a man would shudder to see a human being wear;
and I will dim those eyes, so beautiful before,

312
00:21:37,650 --> 00:21:43,490
so that you seem a vile thing to all the suitors —
and to your wife, and to the son you left in your halls.

313
00:21:43,490 --> 00:21:46,790
And you yourself: go first of all to the swineherd,

314
00:21:46,790 --> 00:21:49,830
the keeper of your pigs —
whose heart is loyal to you all the same,

315
00:21:49,830 --> 00:21:53,010
who loves your boy, and steady-hearted Penelope.

316
00:21:53,010 --> 00:21:56,130
You will find him sitting by the
swine; and they are at pasture

317
00:21:56,130 --> 00:22:01,730
by the Rock of the Raven, at the spring Arethusa,
eating acorns to their hearts' content and drinking

318
00:22:01,730 --> 00:22:05,590
black water — the things that feed the rich fat on swine.

319
00:22:05,590 --> 00:22:08,870
Stay there; sit by him and question him of everything,

320
00:22:08,870 --> 00:22:12,090
while I go on to Sparta, the country of beautiful women,

321
00:22:12,090 --> 00:22:15,230
to summon Telemachus — your own dear son, Odysseus —

322
00:22:15,230 --> 00:22:18,490
who went to wide-dancing-grounded Lacedaemon, to Menelaus,

323
00:22:18,490 --> 00:22:22,210
seeking news of you, whether
you were still alive anywhere."

324
00:22:22,210 --> 00:22:24,970
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered her:

325
00:22:24,970 --> 00:22:28,530
"Why did you not tell him, then —
you whose mind holds everything?

326
00:22:28,530 --> 00:22:34,010
So that he too might wander in misery over the
barren sea, while other men eat away his living?"

327
00:22:34,010 --> 00:22:36,670
Then the goddess answered him, gray-eyed Athena:

328
00:22:36,670 --> 00:22:39,150
"Do not let him weigh too heavily on your heart.

329
00:22:39,150 --> 00:22:42,110
I myself was his escort, so that he might win himself

330
00:22:42,110 --> 00:22:44,410
a good report by going there. He sits

331
00:22:44,410 --> 00:22:49,130
at his ease in the house of Atreus' son,
with plenty before him.

332
00:22:49,130 --> 00:22:52,370
True — the young men lie in ambush
for him with their black ship,

333
00:22:52,370 --> 00:22:54,770
hungry to kill him before he reaches his fathers' land.

334
00:22:54,770 --> 00:22:59,290
But I do not look for that. No:
sooner will the earth close over

335
00:22:59,290 --> 00:23:02,570
more than one of the suitor-men who eat away your living."

336
00:23:02,570 --> 00:23:05,610
So speaking, Athena touched him with her wand.

337
00:23:05,610 --> 00:23:08,110
She withered the fine skin on his supple limbs;

338
00:23:08,110 --> 00:23:13,790
she stripped the fair hair from his head; and about
all his limbs she set the skin of an aged old man;

339
00:23:13,790 --> 00:23:19,690
and she dimmed those eyes, so beautiful before,
and threw about him a foul rag and a tunic —

340
00:23:19,690 --> 00:23:22,870
tattered things, filthy, black with reeking smoke —

341
00:23:22,870 --> 00:23:26,190
and over them she dressed him in
the great hide of a swift deer,

342
00:23:26,190 --> 00:23:30,270
worn bald. And she gave him a
staff, and a miserable pouch,

343
00:23:30,270 --> 00:23:33,750
ragged full of holes, slung on a rope of twisted cord.

344
00:23:33,750 --> 00:23:37,330
So the two of them, their plot laid, parted. And she

345
00:23:37,330 --> 00:23:41,400
went off to shining Lacedaemon, after the son of Odysseus.
